# 06. Observation

An Observation represents information received from a source.

Examples include:

```text
environmental observation
patient-related observation
facility observation
laboratory observation
manual observation
system-generated observation
```

An Observation is not automatically a threat.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Observation
├── id
├── source
├── observedAt
├── location
├── subject
├── measurement/context
├── quality
├── provenance
└── createdAt
```

The exact measurement schema depends on the source domain.

AEGIS must not assume every observation has identical fields.

---

# 07. Signal

A Signal represents structured information derived from one or more observations.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Signal
├── id
├── type
├── sourceReferences
├── detectedAt
├── location
├── severity/context
├── confidence
├── status
├── metadata
└── provenance
```

A Signal is not equivalent to a confirmed threat.

---

# 08. Anomaly

An Anomaly represents a signal or pattern that deviates sufficiently from an expected condition to require attention.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Anomaly
├── id
├── signalReferences
├── detectedAt
├── location
├── description
├── severity
├── confidence
├── status
├── detectionMethod
├── supportingEvidence
└── timestamps
```

The anomaly lifecycle must remain distinct from the threat lifecycle.

---

# 09. Event

An Event represents the operational occurrence that AEGIS is tracking.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Event
├── id
├── type
├── title
├── status
├── detectedAt
├── occurredAt
├── location
├── severity
├── description
├── sourceReferences
├── evidenceReferences
├── threatReferences
├── riskReferences
├── createdBy
└── timestamps
```

An Event may connect multiple domain entities.

---

# 10. Threat

A Threat represents the operational threat context associated with an event.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Threat
├── id
├── eventReferences
├── classification
├── status
├── confidence
├── severity
├── evidenceReferences
├── investigationReferences
├── riskAssessmentReferences
├── transmissionAssessmentReferences
└── timestamps
```

Critical rule:

> **Threat classification is not the same thing as threat certainty.**

The model must preserve uncertainty.

---

# 11. Threat Classification

Classification must be represented independently from confidence.

For example:

```text
classification
confidence
evidence
status
```

must not be collapsed into one field.

This allows AEGIS to represent:

```text
Classification = Category A
Confidence = Low
Status = Under Review
```

without falsely representing the threat as confirmed.

The exact controlled vocabulary must be defined by the domain/governance layer.

AI agents must not invent pathogen classification systems.

---

# 12. RiskAssessment

RiskAssessment represents a structured assessment of operational risk.

Conceptual structure:

```text
RiskAssessment
├── id
├── targetType
├── targetId
├── status
├── severity
├── likelihood
├── impact
├── confidence
├── rationale
├── evidenceReferences
├── assessedBy
├── assessedAt
├── validFrom
├── validUntil
└── timestamps
```

The actual scoring methodology must be configurable rather than hard-coded into UI components.

---

# 13. Risk History

Risk must be historically reconstructable.

Therefore, a new assessment should not simply overwrite the previous assessment.

Conceptually:

```text
Risk Assessment 01
        ↓
Risk Assessment 02
        ↓
Risk Assessment 03
        ↓
Current Assessment
```

Each assessment retains:

```text
actor
time
rationale
evidence
result
```

---

# 14. Investigation

Investigation represents a structured process of examining an event, threat, or case.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Investigation
├── id
├── targetReferences
├── status
├── objective
├── hypotheses
├── findingReferences
├── evidenceReferences
├── assignedActors
├── startedAt
├── completedAt
└── timestamps
```

---

# 15. Investigation Hypothesis

A hypothesis must preserve uncertainty.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Hypothesis
├── id
├── statement
├── status
├── confidence
├── supportingEvidence
├── contradictingEvidence
├── createdBy
├── createdAt
├── updatedAt
└── history
```

Possible conceptual states:

```text
Proposed
Under Review
Supported
Contradicted
Rejected
Unresolved
```

These values are subject to final domain governance.

---

# 16. Case

A Case represents an individual operational investigation unit.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Case
├── id
├── eventReferences
├── subjectReference
├── location
├── status
├── timelineReferences
├── evidenceReferences
├── findingReferences
├── relationshipReferences
├── assignedActors
└── timestamps
```

AEGIS must avoid storing sensitive information that is not required for the system's operational purpose.

---

# 17. Finding

A Finding represents a structured conclusion or observation produced during investigation.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Finding
├── id
├── investigationId
├── type
├── statement
├── confidence
├── evidenceReferences
├── author
├── createdAt
├── updatedAt
└── status
```

A Finding must remain traceable to its evidence.

---

# 18. Evidence

Evidence represents information supporting an operational or analytical claim.

Conceptual structure:

```text
Evidence
├── id
├── type
├── source
├── reference
├── collectedAt
├── observedAt
├── reliability
├── provenance
├── integrity
├── relatedEntities
└── timestamps
```

Evidence must not be confused with interpretation.

```text
Evidence
      ↓
Interpretation
      ↓
Finding
      ↓
Assessment
```

These are separate concepts.

---

# 19. Evidence Provenance

Every meaningful evidence object should preserve provenance where available.

Conceptually:

```text
WHO / WHAT
     ↓
WHERE FROM
     ↓
WHEN
     ↓
HOW ACQUIRED
     ↓
INTEGRITY / QUALITY
```

This is essential for high-stakes environments.

---

# 20. TransmissionAssessment

TransmissionAssessment represents the system's structured understanding of possible relationships or pathways.

Conceptual structure:

```text
TransmissionAssessment
├── id
├── sourceReferences
├── targetReferences
├── pathway
├── evidenceReferences
├── confidence
├── status
├── interventionPoints
├── assessedBy
└── timestamps
```

The system must distinguish:

```text
observed relationship
suspected relationship
inferred relationship
confirmed relationship
rejected relationship
```

where applicable.

---

# 21. ContainmentAction

ContainmentAction represents an intervention intended to limit an identified risk.

Conceptual structure:

```text
ContainmentAction
├── id
├── targetReferences
├── type
├── status
├── proposal
├── rationale
├── affectedScope
├── authorization
├── execution
├── verification
├── outcome
├── createdBy
└── timestamps
```

Containment must preserve the distinction between:

```text
Proposed
Authorized
Executing
Active
Verified
Failed
Cancelled
Superseded
```

Only states approved by the domain governance should become production enum values.

---

# 22. ResponseAction

ResponseAction represents a broader operational response activity.

Conceptual structure:

```text
ResponseAction
├── id
├── targetReferences
├── type
├── status
├── priority
├── owner
├── proposal
├── authorization
├── execution
├── outcome
