# 35. Entity Relationship Architecture

The core relationship graph is:

```text
Observation
    ↓
Signal
    ↓
Anomaly
    ↓
Event
    ↓
Threat
    ↓
RiskAssessment
    ↓
Investigation
    ↓
Case
    ↓
Finding
    ↓
TransmissionAssessment
    ↓
ContainmentAction
    ↓
ResponseAction
    ↓
RecoveryAssessment
    ↓
PreparednessLesson
    ↓
ResilienceGap
    ↓
ImprovementItem
```

Supporting:

```text
Evidence ───────────────→ almost every analytical entity
Location ────────────────→ events / cases / signals / actions
Actor ───────────────────→ investigations / assessments / actions
ResearchFinding ─────────→ evidence/context
AuditRecord ─────────────→ consequential state changes
DomainEvent ─────────────→ state transitions
Notification ────────────→ relevant operational events
```

---

# 36. Relationship Rule

Relationships must be explicit.

Do not encode complex relationships merely through strings.

Bad:

```text
relatedCases: "C001,C002,C003"
```

Preferred conceptual model:

```text
CaseRelationship
├── sourceCase
├── targetCase
├── relationshipType
├── confidence
├── evidenceReferences
├── status
└── timestamps
```

---

# 37. Current State vs History

AEGIS must maintain a distinction between:

```text
CURRENT STATE
```

and:

```text
STATE HISTORY
```

Example:

```text
Threat
Current Status = Under Review
```

does not mean previous states disappear.

History should preserve:

```text
Candidate
 ↓
Under Review
 ↓
Characterized
```

with corresponding events/audit records.

---

# 38. Temporal Data Principle

Important operational data should carry temporal information.

At minimum where relevant:

```text
occurredAt
observedAt
createdAt
updatedAt
startedAt
completedAt
```

Do not collapse all temporal concepts into:

```text
timestamp
```

when different meanings exist.

---

# 39. Identity Rules

Every persisted domain entity must have a stable identifier.

Conceptually:

```text
entity.id
```

Identifiers must:

- be unique
- remain stable
- not depend on UI position
- not be generated from display names
- not change when the entity is renamed

---

# 40. Display Identifier vs Internal Identifier

Where operationally useful, AEGIS may expose human-readable identifiers.

Example:

```text
Internal ID:
UUID

Display ID:
AEGIS-THR-001
```

The display identifier must not become the database identity.

---

# 41. Enumerations

Controlled values must be centralized.

Examples:

```text
ThreatStatus
RiskStatus
InvestigationStatus
CaseStatus
ActionStatus
RecoveryStatus
NotificationSeverity
EvidenceType
ActorType
```

No screen should independently define its own version of an enum.

Bad:

```text
ThreatOverview:
"active"

ThreatDiscovery:
"ACTIVE"

Executive:
"ongoing"
```

Instead:

```text
Canonical enum
      ↓
All consumers
```

---

# 42. Unknown and Uncertain Values

AEGIS must represent uncertainty explicitly.

Avoid forcing:

```text
unknown
```

into:

```text
false
```

or:

```text
not available
```

into:

```text
zero
```

Examples:

```text
confidence = unknown
risk = not_assessed
classification = unresolved
```

are conceptually different states.

---

# 43. Null Semantics

Null must have a defined meaning.

Possible meanings include:

```text
not collected
not applicable
not yet assessed
temporarily unavailable
unknown
redacted
```

These must not be treated as interchangeable.

The final backend contract must define where explicit status fields are preferable to null values.

---

# 44. Data Provenance

Data must preserve where it came from.

Conceptually:

```text
Source
 ↓
Observation
 ↓
Interpretation
 ↓
Assessment
 ↓
Decision
```

The system should be able to trace important claims backward.

---

# 45. Data Lineage

AEGIS should support a lineage chain:

```text
SOURCE
 ↓
OBSERVATION
 ↓
SIGNAL
 ↓
ANOMALY
 ↓
EVENT
 ↓
THREAT
 ↓
ASSESSMENT
 ↓
ACTION
 ↓
OUTCOME
```

This is one of the most important properties of a high-stakes system.

---

# 46. Evidence Does Not Equal Truth

The data architecture must preserve the distinction between:

```text
source data
```

and:

```text
system interpretation
```

For example:

```text
Evidence:
Observed pattern X

Interpretation:
Possible relationship Y

Assessment:
Confidence = moderate
```

The system must not collapse these into:

```text
Confirmed Y
```

without appropriate evidence and authorization.

---

# 47. Frontend Data Model

The frontend should consume normalized domain data and derive display models where necessary.

Conceptually:

```text
API Response
      ↓
Domain Adapter
      ↓
Application State
      ↓
Selector / Derived State
      ↓
View Model
      ↓
Component
```

This prevents presentation-specific data structures from becoming backend truth.

---

# 48. View Model

A view model is allowed to combine data for presentation.

Example:

```text
ThreatOverviewViewModel
├── threat
├── latestRisk
├── activeInvestigation
├── latestEvidence
├── currentActions
└── timeline
```

But:

> A ViewModel is not a new domain entity.

---

# 49. API Contract Principle

APIs should expose explicit contracts.

Avoid endpoints that return arbitrary database objects directly.

Bad conceptual pattern:

```text
GET /everything
```

Preferred:

```text
GET /events/:id
GET /threats/:id
GET /risk-assessments/:id
GET /investigations/:id
GET /cases/:id
```

The exact API surface will be defined in the API architecture document.

---

# 50. Command vs Query

AEGIS should conceptually distinguish:

## Query

Requests information.

```text
GET /threats/:id
```

## Command

Requests a state-changing action.

```text
START_INVESTIGATION
AUTHORIZE_CONTAINMENT
COMPLETE_RESPONSE
```

A command is not simply a database update.

It represents an intended domain action.

---

# 51. Command Contract

Conceptually:

```text
Command
├── commandId
├── commandType
├── actor
├── target
├── payload
├── reason
├── correlationId
└── timestamp
```

Example:

```text
START_INVESTIGATION

target:
Threat T-001

actor:
Actor A-001

reason:
Operational review required
```

---

# 52. Command Processing

```text
COMMAND
 ↓
AUTHENTICATION
 ↓
AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
VALIDATION
 ↓
DOMAIN RULES
 ↓
STATE TRANSITION
 ↓
PERSISTENCE
 ↓
DOMAIN EVENT
 ↓
AUDIT
 ↓
RESPONSE
```

---

# 53. API Response Contract

Successful response:

```text
{
  "data": {},
  "meta": {}
}
```

Error response:

```text
{
  "error": {
    "code": "",
    "message": "",
    "details": {}
  }
}
```

The exact production schema will be standardized in the API specification.

---

# 54. Error Contract

Errors must be machine-readable.

Examples:

```text
INVALID_TRANSITION
UNAUTHORIZED_ACTION
VALIDATION_FAILED
ENTITY_NOT_FOUND
CONFLICT
PERSISTENCE_FAILURE
DEPENDENCY_UNAVAILABLE
STALE_DATA
RATE_LIMITED
SYSTEM_UNAVAILABLE
```

The UI should translate these into appropriate human-readable feedback.

---

# 55. Optimistic Concurrency

For consequential entities, the system should support detection of stale updates.

Conceptually:

```text
Entity Version = 7
```

User submits update based on:

```text
Version = 7
```

But server now has:

```text
Version = 8
```

The operation should not silently overwrite Version 8.

Instead:

```text
CONFLICT
```

must be returned and handled explicitly.

---

# 56. Idempotency

Consequential commands should support idempotency where appropriate.

Example:

```text
AUTHORIZE_CONTAINMENT
commandId = CMD-123
```

If the same command is submitted twice:

```text
CMD-123
CMD-123
```

the system must not accidentally perform two independent operations.

---

# 57. Real-Time Data Contract

Real-time updates should use domain events rather than arbitrary UI messages.

Conceptually:

```text
Domain Event
 ↓
Event Transport
 ↓
Subscribed Client
 ↓
State Update
 ↓
Affected UI
```

Example:

```text
THREAT_STATUS_UPDATED
```

may cause:

```text
Threat Overview
Risk Overview
Executive View
Notifications
```

to update.

