# 71. Deletion Principle

AEGIS should avoid destructive deletion of consequential operational records.

Prefer:

```text
Archived
Cancelled
Rejected
Superseded
Resolved
```

where appropriate.

Historical traceability must survive state changes.

---

# 72. Referential Integrity

Relationships between entities must remain valid.

For example:

```text
Threat
 ↓
RiskAssessment
```

must not reference a non-existent threat.

The system must enforce relationship integrity at the appropriate layer.

---

# 73. Orphan Prevention

The system must prevent orphaned operational records.

Examples:

```text
Finding → missing Investigation
Action → missing Target
AuditRecord → missing Entity reference
Lesson → missing Source Event
```

Where historical records reference deleted or archived entities, the architecture must define explicit archival/reference behavior.

---

# 74. Data Versioning

Important domain structures should be versioned when changes could affect interpretation.

Conceptually:

```text
Schema Version
Domain Model Version
Assessment Method Version
Event Contract Version
```

This becomes especially important as AEGIS evolves.

---

# 75. Backward Compatibility

When API contracts change:

```text
Old Client
+
New Backend
```

must not unexpectedly break.

Breaking changes require:

```text
versioning
migration
or coordinated deployment
```

---

# 76. Seed / Demo Data

During development, AEGIS may use synthetic data.

Synthetic data must be clearly identifiable.

Example conceptual marker:

```text
